  <h2 id="seats">The seat arithmetic, using current numbers</h2>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">1,40,664 MBBS seats</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">64,121 government, 76,543 private</div>
      <p>NMC's revised matrix (21 September 2026) covers 839 colleges: 447 government and 392 private. It excludes institutes of national importance such as AIIMS. The July matrix showed 1,36,939. Private seats are 54% of the total.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">11.21 lakh qualified</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">About 8 qualifiers per seat</div>
      <p>11.21 lakh divided by 1,40,664 is about 8. Against government seats alone it is about 17.5. Against the close to 20 lakh who sat the test, it is about 14 for every MBBS seat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">90,780 scored 500 or more</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">More than the 64,121 government seats</div>
      <p>NTA counts 10,160 at 600 or more and 90,780 at 500 or more. Many of those students also want BDS and AYUSH, so government seats go well before the 500 mark in the all-India list.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">About 28,000 BDS seats</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">About 85.5% are private</div>
      <p>The figure Careers360 cites from the Dental Council of India is 28,016 seats in 329 colleges: 4,058 government and 23,958 private. A second Careers360 matrix shows 27,868.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      Vacant seats do not mean cheap seats. Odisha's 313 vacancies after round 2 included 139 private MBBS and 78 private BDS seats alongside government seats in district colleges. Ask for the fee, the bond and the college record before you take one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The Government told Parliament in August 2025 that India has about 13,86,157 registered allopathic doctors, a ratio of 1 to 811 on an 80% availability assumption. That means the national count is not thin. The gap is local, and specialist training is the scarce part.
  </p>

